#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0b4da9-1a0f-5a46-aff1-3ce5c0cebb3b" :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c77b2f36-6be8-50c0-ae22-868fa6cf86a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401022} {:answer/id #uuid "09d3bae4-8fa4-5410-960c-88cdf9ccbafc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401023} {:answer/id #uuid "e29d1f8d-e673-5fab-ab5a-b94d3c279c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401024} {:answer/id #uuid "d634400e-7fcc-5b2f-8882-b33255d21d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401025} {:answer/id #uuid "33483e89-3247-5b5b-b6c3-9a62773834b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c77b2f36-6be8-50c0-ae22-868fa6cf86a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401022} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d94100e-63ab-58cf-b2c0-0f485eafe140" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a434871-4fea-52cd-80a4-1fd81c159fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-15133" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a760f55b6437a39010e4d1a00bdb5fb5390d848dd74347e7736e2ac49cd562b.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}+(12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+144{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9({sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta ))}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )+16{cos}^{2}⁡(4\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0b4da9-1a0f-5a46-aff1-3ce5c0cebb3b" :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d63901ad-848e-5f8f-b7f4-a6f0cabcd6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401029} {:answer/id #uuid "802cfcd0-a88b-5f37-b901-56f2fa1fec34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401030} {:answer/id #uuid "3d75caa2-eae0-5f0c-a250-6aa6ab5b7726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401031} {:answer/id #uuid "892fe229-a636-5af2-bb7c-28f7e30053e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401032} {:answer/id #uuid "4d7af40e-74ed-51e2-be9d-1cf5c95c728a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d7af40e-74ed-51e2-be9d-1cf5c95c728a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401033} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aecd8d8-445d-5023-ad24-6e7f3a0a5ec4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c659d13d-a024-51d5-b83b-b004bef94416" :question/math-academy-id "q-15136" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+{e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(1+{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2{e}^{\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0b4da9-1a0f-5a46-aff1-3ce5c0cebb3b" :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbc2c061-b810-50f4-998c-b4c24f1e568e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+4\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401035} {:answer/id #uuid "2da42875-db42-5de8-9dff-ef55f911995f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401036} {:answer/id #uuid "1d0f9249-e37d-56d1-8c90-4ef1bb8df2ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{4{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401037} {:answer/id #uuid "21b19373-36de-5c05-9a8d-58dcde77b9ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401038} {:answer/id #uuid "293c2bba-fb9f-5a3b-a596-e4fc57546fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "293c2bba-fb9f-5a3b-a596-e4fc57546fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401039} :answer-field/id #uuid "61740183-1d83-54ca-8ff3-51d0ccdbf570" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd646452-89ff-5f38-8817-208b877e41c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-15144" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b03822d65ec9f5bea3a1e47d25e4831470754fa09cef510e7c452cc292e9e40.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={e}^{\\theta }+\\theta {e}^{\\theta }=(1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\theta {e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{((1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }+(1+2\\theta +{\\theta }^{2}){e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1){e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b03546f-6c79-53b9-b284-fb23ae4dba52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "925080f9-1488-5ed3-9988-57bac8e25aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+2t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401042} {:answer/id #uuid "8b14ae74-d72b-5c6f-bfb4-9fdf19b4b592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401043} {:answer/id #uuid "c911c453-c1b3-506d-a7de-c8341e32d5e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401044} {:answer/id #uuid "69aeb839-370f-503b-8dde-d870cf822808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401045} {:answer/id #uuid "e1915447-2ced-5598-8af5-d918f3a8a810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+24t+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c911c453-c1b3-506d-a7de-c8341e32d5e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401044} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac60323-fbe0-5435-bfb2-619a71c7a175" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dddf8a10-d360-5144-aae4-5bd6e2bd6915" :question/math-academy-id "q-15174" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})=3{t}^{2}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})=3{t}^{2}-2t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{(3{t}^{2}+2t)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2}-2t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+12{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}-12{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{18{t}^{4}+8{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b85309c-59d7-5810-84c4-7ea68f827ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401049} {:answer/id #uuid "cdd04e4b-26ba-5e0d-9091-43c5f06e6bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401050} {:answer/id #uuid "eb350998-6cb3-5e21-b128-0e21ffcf76a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401051} {:answer/id #uuid "9f297950-b005-5b22-b845-7190b859cf3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401052} {:answer/id #uuid "a99448ba-c640-5e57-a298-7dcd50a71e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb350998-6cb3-5e21-b128-0e21ffcf76a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401051} :answer-field/id #uuid "9286f2bc-6874-57c4-a822-f4b4566f80e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a2f2890-829e-5660-aa0e-71dea52c1f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-15178" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca290e8fd51edddf4381ec267b8e0a5692d0e52bd2a36b7d43ab89cbeb5cf00d.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41c46a2e-4e00-5991-99c9-9dc68c9a9598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401056} {:answer/id #uuid "2499ce46-f979-5a96-92be-fd0c7ac31bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401057} {:answer/id #uuid "61eb1149-6ab5-5e3a-8377-404854e1b082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401058} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed903c6-dd0e-5b89-95d6-7cb6b868a842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401059} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b764bb-1a8d-5c44-bf30-3132c5443550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2499ce46-f979-5a96-92be-fd0c7ac31bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401057} :answer-field/id #uuid "d340f787-388c-5296-b3a4-86e39060db0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "243b404d-a4f2-57e8-bc56-d916439edf0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-15182" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d683c3960705fa32d11826008a9844c51c8e59e38f5158fce7f4e44c31b97ca9.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+9{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "606fd559-6309-5ad0-9d39-b14bac651a0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401063} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7d81c0-3670-53fa-96c1-92db4f5667c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401064} {:answer/id #uuid "b70dd52d-4f80-5155-8124-3b4a4b1be40d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401065} {:answer/id #uuid "0d34d36a-3d69-5d6d-803b-25c205cff3c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401066} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c20d18-1824-50eb-b1e3-665bfefc7f00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6c20d18-1824-50eb-b1e3-665bfefc7f00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401067} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3dd9699-b980-5dfd-8981-2e277c08e891" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "676c520e-2bd4-5a9f-a56a-7aedf1c83e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-15323" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ on $[1,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bda136-40a9-576a-b85c-4d4ebefaeb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bead1cb0-40bc-5682-8aea-b1306552b221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401070} {:answer/id #uuid "86056585-0e06-5b86-b0c0-36b6f0375c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401071} {:answer/id #uuid "7c15ff06-b974-57e0-9d0d-91476444df9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401072} {:answer/id #uuid "552b9d8c-6c80-5fa5-9717-bf514c6bbbca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401073} {:answer/id #uuid "65a93180-72f9-5046-97f5-9cb178f53496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65a93180-72f9-5046-97f5-9cb178f53496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401074} :answer-field/id #uuid "4182a726-9587-5280-b0ae-9a47c4e9e265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16fc9f31-33f6-5972-9529-3afa5f1e1dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-15465" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1+0} \\\\  & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bda136-40a9-576a-b85c-4d4ebefaeb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b60199c4-dc25-5568-af40-de39cd26589d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401077} {:answer/id #uuid "41c812bd-cb3b-5fc5-aef0-fb585602e943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401078} {:answer/id #uuid "ae37cc4a-dbda-5fe8-9ffa-a6b2a950bdbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401079} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fc02f7-9666-528e-9191-ebdcde2135fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401080} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea8ed64-c406-5164-b57d-847952b47323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae37cc4a-dbda-5fe8-9ffa-a6b2a950bdbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401079} :answer-field/id #uuid "afbc7c1c-c03b-5382-bd6b-5a9beec491d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f9e0331-ffe6-5489-b436-faf1f0bea590" :question/math-academy-id "q-15469" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}+1,\\,y={t}^{4}+2{t}^{2},\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4t({t}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{4t({t}^{2}+1)}{6{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({t}^{2}+1)}{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2((1)^{2}+1)}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6f0c89e-fe19-5aaf-9f85-b5f0ff7a344e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d256b930-0dee-50c4-bd81-41d64217fa7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,4-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401084} {:answer/id #uuid "2759b4ff-6fe0-5744-9043-700096ff43f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,6-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,6+\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401085} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3a7f86-7353-5871-ab3c-151f2b4eb6c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,5-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,5+\\sqrt{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401086} {:answer/id #uuid "4fc673dc-55ce-532c-a658-37120012f1ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,5-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,5+\\sqrt{5})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401087} {:answer/id #uuid "01d470c7-780c-5f9a-a462-fc902c6b68dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,4-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{5})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01d470c7-780c-5f9a-a462-fc902c6b68dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,4-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{5})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401088} :answer-field/id #uuid "62252cab-6046-5d37-8592-a5f805f44339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b39c00d5-06a8-557a-8fa0-abcc975f62c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-15692" :question/problem "![](images/18/185393f07b920bf9b72799f1c17c4245017b500ff1ca92a0104fe95ce621bde4.png)\n\nFind the $y$-intercepts of the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=9,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (0-2)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ 4+(y-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =9-4 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =5 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts occur at the points $(0,4-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{5}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b85acdfc-0505-5472-b5f3-53270bffa030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the y-Axis or z-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bbc1cc8-8336-57ba-beda-f232f5e9222e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401091} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7b05bc-51cf-52c8-a8de-4a77f3929ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨y,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401092} {:answer/id #uuid "56289067-d38c-5f93-8493-5a4b64b6dbc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\pi y,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401093} {:answer/id #uuid "b49fe862-0b93-571b-b320-daffcc013018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401094} {:answer/id #uuid "94ddc772-4e3c-53f3-95c3-4e3a3d691c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨y,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b49fe862-0b93-571b-b320-daffcc013018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401094} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f2a13be-e010-5c10-884d-b42e9a33ee3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "428cd283-358d-5b3a-86aa-024fc5c3885c" :question/math-academy-id "q-156978" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $r(y,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={sin}^{2}⁡y$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Which of the following could be $r?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6fb27c0d050a7007e9f4a0d526daaff40b8f7d01f20c63ac8cc00074121f28.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta =[0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(y)={sin}^{2}⁡y\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,{sin}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [0,\\pi ],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7d226d3-f7d3-574f-94f9-a532ec99b0ac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Expression for a Least-Squares Solution in Terms of the Matrices Q and R"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f67bd72e-fdc1-5a03-8447-bfd09a70f376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401098} {:answer/id #uuid "01a67471-d12d-5153-bc2d-d06208bd68e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401099} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dbeaf3-ad0e-5cbe-a0fc-7d2689833b25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401100} {:answer/id #uuid "66035904-de9d-5004-9133-f65b6dd564b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and ${Q}^{T}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401101} {:answer/id #uuid "f8434c0c-cb29-5697-82e4-05952a54d5fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and ${R}^{T}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66035904-de9d-5004-9133-f65b6dd564b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and ${Q}^{T}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401101} :answer-field/id #uuid "474be29c-51a5-5850-8515-5dee1fd67caa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f81ecce3-b49e-5261-aee2-21f0b16a1f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-157052" :question/problem "$$\n\\boxed{}\\,x=\\boxed{}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ R\\,\\,x & ={Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $R$ and ${Q}^{T}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7d226d3-f7d3-574f-94f9-a532ec99b0ac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Expression for a Least-Squares Solution in Terms of the Matrices Q and R"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fdcd9d7-d7a2-5fbc-b814-a5cdbab0aba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{-1}$ and ${Q}^{T}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401105} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac28ba0-e324-56db-ad83-179ae52ae559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{-1}$ and $Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401106} {:answer/id #uuid "7921d298-b867-528e-8983-2fecc690fc56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401107} {:answer/id #uuid "b00b89ef-c0e1-594a-b11a-06139f3f787e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and ${Q}^{T}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401108} {:answer/id #uuid "7148d690-eb6d-590f-9666-9f4226696830" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and ${R}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fdcd9d7-d7a2-5fbc-b814-a5cdbab0aba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{-1}$ and ${Q}^{T}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401105} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d1a3359-653b-5fbe-99d8-82db0139d073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d32f936e-03bc-5f70-a00c-2926fd51fe56" :question/math-academy-id "q-158036" :question/problem "$$\nx=\\boxed{}\\,\\boxed{}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & =\\,\\,\\,{R}^{-1}\\,\\,{Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are ${R}^{-1}$ and ${Q}^{T}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e44305e5-b7e3-5ae2-925c-9329f37823b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Circle with a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c893f0-7ed9-50d0-9479-4bfb6c5c6906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401112} {:answer/id #uuid "5e681a2e-8cd8-5ca9-b5d3-8777f4728c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401113} {:answer/id #uuid "075c1360-2993-54ea-a5ab-60fe6486955e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401114} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1e5ed6-4c6c-5020-bbc5-7c83a63fb116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401115} {:answer/id #uuid "d396b6f8-3098-5eff-a463-bdfe23d65067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e681a2e-8cd8-5ca9-b5d3-8777f4728c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401113} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c104c61-200a-5174-998d-683d5dd55c83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cfa05974-d33e-5f3c-90f3-bf0b329bfc3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15805" :question/problem "![](images/de/de12c0b5be8318cc21c62615d24125998c22d55d37abafdee37c103c3840cb71.png)\n\nFind the points where the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=20$ intersects the line $x-3y=2$ (sketched above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation $x-3y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}+4)}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{10}{3})}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{10{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{9}+\\frac{100}{9} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+10 & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $x=-4,2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-values. Start with $x=-4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first intersection point is $(-4,-2).$ Next, we plug in $x=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intersection point is $(2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the line $x-3y=2$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=20$ at the points $(-4,-2)$ and $(2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8042759c-34e5-5005-b223-6ae432924393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401118} {:answer/id #uuid "005523ea-9cd4-5f90-a22e-334d6a25f40b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401119} {:answer/id #uuid "9fcc1510-f906-5cf2-aee6-a593897199ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<y<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401120} {:answer/id #uuid "54b76232-1674-5242-9acf-123f7bdcf11e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<y<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401121} {:answer/id #uuid "3512bb23-ec85-559a-bb44-2c0d0c3ebdfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le y\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b76232-1674-5242-9acf-123f7bdcf11e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<y<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401121} :answer-field/id #uuid "071a160d-4402-56e8-b9f3-e34425bb2388" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "846d6059-ad4f-5e01-8a65-610c7d23e3a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-158314" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b95c538613ee8672e0f1cac970b7af230ec40bf3075e609b1e03f7cad4382fb1.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<1,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/19/19e8e52881066864cba562ed2a8cdc2944b9dae416c7124fdee207cdad1d8bdf.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/90/907e996fb7aea36e0c924feb0f5d9dc6804c768be775953d277e84b0145ef5a0.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<1,\\,-1<y<0}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a5f50b7-1c4b-5f7a-b602-f12beaae89e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=49\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401124} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c1e0e9-11c9-5b29-a0fa-ad459d001600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401125} {:answer/id #uuid "c7046323-aec7-5353-9ec7-55a1fb762409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401126} {:answer/id #uuid "12304df3-e4be-5122-93c7-3516406cb605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401127} {:answer/id #uuid "7476a25d-d04e-55b7-83f1-d52ec7ba798e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12304df3-e4be-5122-93c7-3516406cb605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401127} :answer-field/id #uuid "500e2e4d-f73d-516b-9ffd-1e74864efbb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46c88046-0f62-50bb-8a31-ae13a6c535ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-15856" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c46972a3968d7110cb08d4b16bfff9267e49b102cf005cdbb21a3630d15c49d.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $7$ and vertical radius $2,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=7$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f2bc84-03e8-5921-81e2-683a09195279" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Function Containing Exponential, Logarithmic, and Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d17359d3-8c16-5f6a-b127-4635699145de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ne 2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401131} {:answer/id #uuid "723d1468-5b49-57b7-aca8-480bc0d17b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401132} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee0d3a5-a4a1-5cee-a062-442dab6411d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401133} {:answer/id #uuid "ab4e594f-0e4b-5943-92bb-bf8c40588e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401134} {:answer/id #uuid "20fdbcce-ba79-5860-9e27-a2583b78aa49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge 2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab4e594f-0e4b-5943-92bb-bf8c40588e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The domain is the entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401134} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a4d4ae3-6796-52c1-a3b7-76a9cd48622e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3d7c34b-eed1-5181-a45d-9a598cc7cc31" :question/math-academy-id "q-158569" :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)={2}^{y-2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- $y-2x$ is defined for all $x$ and $y,$ and\n\n- ${2}^{g(x,y)}$ is defined if $g(x,y)$ is defined.\n\nAs a result, ${2}^{y-2x}$ is defined for all values of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the domain of $f$ is the entire $xy$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac9b6b53-5c92-51b1-b36f-2a3cc651f319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 17 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 15 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 8 & 25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401138} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcb6806-97c3-5c5b-a067-e8db96db6a51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401139} {:answer/id #uuid "c58e92d6-356e-5987-82a0-c4ca864d02f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 17 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 15 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401140} {:answer/id #uuid "2efd5c91-0f33-5144-8814-8a5bf4712f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401141} {:answer/id #uuid "47e7f5fa-1b69-57b5-bdb7-eab59dcc0807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 22 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 17 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 15 & 9 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 13 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 25 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2efd5c91-0f33-5144-8814-8a5bf4712f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401141} :answer-field/id #uuid "55328507-f46f-5e80-84e7-a9cf12738872" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "646237af-1831-56a9-a4f0-279fefdaeaf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-159730" :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$22$** | **$17$** | **$15$** | **$11$** | **$8$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$5$** | **$6$** | **$9$** | **$13$** | **$25$** |\n| $y$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-6$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.  Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$22$** | **$17$** | **$15$** | **$11$** | **$8$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$5$** | **$6$** | **$9$** | **$13$** | **$25$** |\n| $y$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-6$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane  \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 17 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 15 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 8 & 25\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf99414e-f1cf-528f-adbb-407cf6e82c4c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Linear Transformation Represented by a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ad4e4e-04cb-51fe-abe1-4e867e431a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Reflection in the line $y=x+\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401145} {:answer/id #uuid "5a15a75a-e128-599b-b3b9-5645e7305697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Reflection in the line $y=\\frac{2}{3}x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401146} {:answer/id #uuid "f0452ac4-63df-52cb-a5ba-4c3d6f3508db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dilation with scale factor $\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401147} {:answer/id #uuid "4708c967-b8d8-54c4-ada0-7e3e2fab1f49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dilation with scale factor $\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401148} {:answer/id #uuid "d94c5e9f-ee52-566e-9640-baf243e6ea0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Reflection in the line $y=\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0452ac4-63df-52cb-a5ba-4c3d6f3508db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dilation with scale factor $\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401147} :answer-field/id #uuid "7629eca5-066a-5cf0-88fc-0ed79060b5bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0bb92e93-2cdf-508a-a231-165363849a16" :question/math-academy-id "q-159764" :question/problem "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=\\frac{2}{3}.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85936915-4c9c-572c-af99-025fec0f7570" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Image of a Line Segment Under the Action of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136e59b4-89b9-509f-b353-97e57960580a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-5,7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401152} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f7fd7a-68ed-5ca3-93c6-f651afc3e675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-2)$ and $(5,-7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401153} {:answer/id #uuid "ff57b12a-81c8-5a7a-bb27-04eafe1c6040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(-5,7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401154} {:answer/id #uuid "5f869935-6196-536e-a49b-7e27025831a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-2)$ and $(-5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401155} {:answer/id #uuid "bad06300-be92-5a4d-91eb-84900e7f65b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "136e59b4-89b9-509f-b353-97e57960580a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-5,7)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401152} :answer-field/id #uuid "51865f85-e4e0-52bf-a6e9-60f97627cefe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9f71d03-299c-533e-bd5f-2dfe8453c281" :question/math-academy-id "q-159797" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ has endpoints at $A(1,1)$ and $B(-1,2).$ What are the coordinates of the corresponding endpoints of $\\overset{―}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}}$, the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{AB}:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective endpoints of the image are ${A}^{′}(2,2)$ and ${B}^{′}(-5,7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fe5d8aa-8a79-5de4-8566-1432a640d78f" :knowledge-point/title "Equating Real and Imaginary Parts of a Complex Number to Solve For an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b234a49-b204-5ed4-bfaa-41d6080ef1a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{4},b=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401159} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6d77b0-4258-5c91-8afc-7ef838e14fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\sqrt{3},b=-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401160} {:answer/id #uuid "d2159356-f202-541b-9892-eebd1e60d1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{4},b=-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401161} {:answer/id #uuid "d6145a7d-35ad-5386-85f1-fee66f6da6b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\sqrt{3},b=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401162} {:answer/id #uuid "5360b829-a541-532f-8d6c-81155ed6dcd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{4},b=\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5360b829-a541-532f-8d6c-81155ed6dcd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{4},b=\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401163} :answer-field/id #uuid "76c64cb5-e76d-53eb-a0e8-6195df2d94ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78111624-f670-5df8-b6e2-bb5a5dde19ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-160013" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{3}{4}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}=a+b\\text{i}$, calculate the value of $a$ and the value of $b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we equate the real parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & =a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we equate the imaginary parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $b=\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bbe2fe7-b69c-54f3-9f1c-3369e3f1395d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Unknowns By Computing a Product and Equating Real and Imaginary Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71361279-b548-5a5f-a16d-dcbf0424a5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2$, $q=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401166} {:answer/id #uuid "76723f95-9757-537d-ba64-6f70bb2a0084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=8$, $q=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401167} {:answer/id #uuid "2783fb88-e21b-5c34-a806-1f0e4be8a043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=-4$, $q=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401168} {:answer/id #uuid "f9389cd2-410b-5bd7-bb2d-afd430456c2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=-3$, $q=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401169} {:answer/id #uuid "6176aa1c-2946-59f3-b638-214d03147fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2$, $q=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76723f95-9757-537d-ba64-6f70bb2a0084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=8$, $q=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401167} :answer-field/id #uuid "e20e0aba-a96a-54f7-bcde-ebba07a66ed9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2bd6d81b-eb1e-5f54-9fb0-b04584c1dccf" :question/math-academy-id "q-160037" :question/problem "Given that $3+5\\text{i}=\\frac{p+\\text{i}q}{1-\\text{i}},$ calculate $p$ and $q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $1-\\text{i}$ to get rid of the fraction and isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+5\\text{i} & =\\frac{p+\\text{i}q}{1-\\text{i}} \\\\ (3+5\\text{i})(1-\\text{i}) & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ 3-3\\text{i}+5\\text{i}-5{\\text{i}}^{2} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ 3+5+2\\text{i} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ 8+2\\text{i} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get\n\n$$\n8=p.\n$$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=8$ and $q=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9f40a0e-83f3-5814-b9a1-8b1265fe93cc" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Unknowns By Computing a Quotient and Equating Real and Imaginary Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1d54338-26b9-548b-83c0-2a886b9ea3a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-\\frac{3}{2},q=\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401173} {:answer/id #uuid "3d44482d-1c30-5ad6-854c-691cae820272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{3}{2},q=-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401174} {:answer/id #uuid "1b7850c0-574e-58be-a128-8a2349e114cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{3}{5},q=-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401175} {:answer/id #uuid "e5144ebf-c2b3-5349-b8ce-5873d55100c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{2}{5},q=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401176} {:answer/id #uuid "930309ca-50d5-5254-aa8c-b2c15baae0ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{3}{2},q=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "930309ca-50d5-5254-aa8c-b2c15baae0ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{3}{2},q=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401177} :answer-field/id #uuid "56ae83c2-5ded-53be-a27e-61769cd01afe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "050685d3-106a-556e-94cf-f2ab6b33d435" :question/math-academy-id "q-160049" :question/problem "Given that $5=(p+q\\text{i})(3+\\text{i})$, calculate $p$ and $q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $3+\\text{i}$ to isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(p+q\\text{i})(3+\\text{i}) \\\\ \\frac{5}{3+\\text{i}} & =p+q\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(5)(3-\\text{i})}{(3+\\text{i})(3-\\text{i})} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{15-5\\text{i}}{{3}^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2}} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{15-5\\text{i}}{9+1} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{15-5\\text{i}}{10} & =p+q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i} & =p+q\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $\\frac{3}{2}=p.$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $-\\frac{1}{2}=q.$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $q=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "499fe0bc-77df-53df-8493-7d4c107031d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eafa79bc-674a-58d4-b671-ca48a2d8af37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no equilibrium solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401180} {:answer/id #uuid "bf12bc70-5702-514d-a81e-85a39e9d74bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401181} {:answer/id #uuid "a206451d-8757-5268-a092-52b0e62ff345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y(x)=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401182} {:answer/id #uuid "1237e62c-9ff7-54ad-b0a2-1816c8134906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=-\\pi $ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401183} {:answer/id #uuid "65a7f74e-7edd-5a89-8720-8a9b642df82e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=0$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eafa79bc-674a-58d4-b671-ca48a2d8af37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The equation has no equilibrium solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401180} :answer-field/id #uuid "68ec482b-0fe8-570d-98b4-c811d283be06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a785f16-be14-5d27-b10a-d2d350268268" :question/math-academy-id "q-160429" :question/problem "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2,$ so we attempt solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡c+2 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡c & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, note that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡c|\\le 1$ for all $c.$ Therefore, the above equation has no (real) solutions.\n\nTherefore, the differential equation has no equilibrium points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfe7ddf8-1d90-5732-b4e0-9b6cb53cfed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401187} {:answer/id #uuid "6b355cde-9299-54ff-9082-f28b3a088a6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=-2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401188} {:answer/id #uuid "a417c7cf-6062-5b00-9bc3-e0de419c5534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401189} {:answer/id #uuid "ac39e8c8-f1ca-5eec-9d60-f35deacbe886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=-v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401190} {:answer/id #uuid "0f39009f-2aaf-5fb6-b951-14bb531af992" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f39009f-2aaf-5fb6-b951-14bb531af992" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401191} :answer-field/id #uuid "003d00c4-a45e-580b-a2b2-1a8ab41f3386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6940edb0-4ff9-5079-afa0-d099bcae85dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160465" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{2}+{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)={e}^{u+v}$ and $y(u,v)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & ={e}^{u+v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ 2u & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2v-{e}^{u+v}\\cdot 2u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2{e}^{u+v}(v-u) & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0 \\\\ u & =v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=v$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "879a74f3-cdb2-5fc9-83e0-82f1953090a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=6u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401194} {:answer/id #uuid "b598f6a3-a6bb-5202-8ed8-ac9c42eb7279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the hyperbola $2{v}^{2}-3{u}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401195} {:answer/id #uuid "bca486aa-25ef-5c7c-9609-2ed1a4c5553a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the circle ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}=6.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401196} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3464e3-f53d-53d8-877c-0141084f3eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabola $2v=3{u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401197} {:answer/id #uuid "14c27fbe-cfe3-5924-a55c-3737ccd260a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{3}{2}u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14c27fbe-cfe3-5924-a55c-3737ccd260a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $v=\\frac{3}{2}u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401198} :answer-field/id #uuid "753118cd-0a71-5bb3-aaee-283497ceb3e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f05de000-4a38-5b78-b8b6-ab15cb5a35af" :question/math-academy-id "q-160473" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2} \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3{u}^{2}+2{v}^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}6u & 4v \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6u\\cdot 1-4v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6u-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 6u-4v & =0 \\\\ v & =\\frac{3}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=\\frac{3}{2}u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d96918f5-2a27-5413-8fcd-a6c8060a9342" :knowledge-point/title "Cases When a Square’s Side Is Mapped to a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38b93a84-d633-56d5-acf0-64e865f690ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401201} {:answer/id #uuid "a58ae1f1-f6a6-59d0-9751-fca06c5ac749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401202} {:answer/id #uuid "062653e1-93bc-5e7e-a802-152e2de8a4c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401203} {:answer/id #uuid "d40073c8-1340-52d4-b3a8-b1c6162defe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401204} {:answer/id #uuid "37e2e5c1-32d5-5a19-92b2-99790aa5b623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "062653e1-93bc-5e7e-a802-152e2de8a4c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401203} :answer-field/id #uuid "1398b2db-d522-591e-9af9-65f757fcf9ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f849a8c-d5a9-5291-8dbf-bee7c6e87ffd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160608" :question/problem "![](images/33/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ and its **directed** boundary $∂Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, where $Δ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v-{u}^{2},\\,y=u+v.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side ${S}_{2}$ under the action of $T$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{2}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{2},$ we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(v-{u}^{2},\\,u+v) \\\\  & =(v-{1}^{2},\\,1+v) \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,v+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{2}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v-1,\\,y=v+1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)=t+1,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,1)$ and $(0,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/f3/f304c409cfc62de3d296690c0acd2cec5b9726c03d8a196ed69de36d8ebbefd2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5256297-c928-54e3-bf4d-35f5734f5632" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Surface Integrals Over Parametric Surfaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c6491ad-31df-5c44-8510-45289f4f4d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401207} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2a58c0-318a-559b-8de4-48cf1599122b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401208} {:answer/id #uuid "66a3fc66-6953-52e2-8b17-93c3ff13ddf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv\\sqrt{uv}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401209} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fed922-03a1-59c5-b2d8-bcf5a4514733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401210} {:answer/id #uuid "ecef412c-1fbd-55f7-a2eb-dcc4e3d8418d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecef412c-1fbd-55f7-a2eb-dcc4e3d8418d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401211} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b5472ad-00ad-5af8-bfaf-97021ee46355" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25e44fde-444d-5880-ba2e-43dd4bc039db" :question/math-academy-id "q-160711" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=xyz$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,uv⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,v⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & v \\\\ 0 & 1 & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}0 & v \\\\ 1 & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & v \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-v,\\,-u,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨-v,\\,-u,\\,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-v)^{2}+(-u)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(xyz)\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=v\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =u\\cdot v\\cdot uv \\\\  & ={u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cee874e3-9f03-5ca9-8149-5c55b3896851" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy-yz+2xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401213} {:answer/id #uuid "395d9de5-d538-52d5-94b9-1ccab518693d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+2yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401214} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ff34b0-c12c-5102-97f3-b0e91c2d28e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy+yz+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401215} {:answer/id #uuid "9fdf9b87-84fe-5783-b107-f4fa60e017e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+3yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401216} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b0c95b-6fc9-5c49-82c2-19c95577125c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6b0c95b-6fc9-5c49-82c2-19c95577125c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401217} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8eb37c1-2270-5997-8c21-89c990ae0c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7492e5fe-6cc9-5d4b-8d1f-20d212147cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-160838" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\overset{~}{n},$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\,,\\,\\overset{~}{n}(x,y,z)=⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩\\,,\\,n=\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}\\frac{\\boxed{}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\overset{~}{n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{(2y)^{2}+(-z)^{2}+(2x)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨2y,\\,-z,\\,2x⟩}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2yz-yz+2{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+yz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}+yz}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" :knowledge-point/title "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac04977-1a37-5d3c-9110-290be918a633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401220} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7eb081-9f2c-5f53-a5b0-e0278d5a43e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401221} {:answer/id #uuid "8b485d3f-8ad7-53a4-b507-d079c7107b80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401222} {:answer/id #uuid "0e733731-6884-58f7-9e73-fde236398200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401223} {:answer/id #uuid "a04db056-3485-5f6c-b6b6-d2d4f8e80aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b485d3f-8ad7-53a4-b507-d079c7107b80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401222} :answer-field/id #uuid "09e4fe92-85aa-505b-a8c5-4df7656265ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd6b23cf-0f4d-571e-a46d-8551c4a4d4b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161072" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y={x}^{2}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,1]$ is given above. What is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y={x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={x}^{2},$ and \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={x}^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2x.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨2{x}^{3},\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "419c0982-db99-5289-b52f-a761ea3d1bae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-x-2{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401227} {:answer/id #uuid "d412c461-4939-5aa5-9ddd-df78a8e85bc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401228} {:answer/id #uuid "a93a034f-199c-5c52-af3b-9d0a37596a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401229} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbb580a-2ddc-5e43-a8f4-c46a6440cff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401230} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a49a16-7b19-549f-8e35-18199cc77301" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfbb580a-2ddc-5e43-a8f4-c46a6440cff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401230} :answer-field/id #uuid "a21eed7a-f07f-5d01-b24f-3b6ba15f83ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd68aee4-128c-55a1-8849-b847e40933c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161198" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2{z}^{2}-x+2(y-{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y-x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2y-x}{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{S}2y-x\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed482a3a-a562-53c9-9952-b7f4ae64a9f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401234} {:answer/id #uuid "292ba469-4e9c-5812-b40c-813e27620124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3y}(-{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401235} {:answer/id #uuid "1139e147-84fe-5bb5-a30a-874f5c401de8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401236} {:answer/id #uuid "25a694b0-85e4-50a9-9c0d-fb4f6db18268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401237} {:answer/id #uuid "60287cb2-73a4-5d70-9afa-49321e3ddf3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3x}({x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25a694b0-85e4-50a9-9c0d-fb4f6db18268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401237} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb2d063b-2ed3-5177-a74e-32306bee1975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8e5c369-0d01-5527-87a6-9d6d08f8cf0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161314" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-x+y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-1)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y(-x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\sqrt{3}(-{x}^{3}y+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fef4addc-f714-5c73-ba79-b8bbf017a6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{4}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401241} {:answer/id #uuid "32ada5ea-3508-5fad-8e67-d2275d868693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{4}{y}^{4}+{x}^{4}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401242} {:answer/id #uuid "0adb517f-11c6-5796-89c8-e402d79f5011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401243} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c7988a-9786-511c-9fd8-d31cb890bb80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-4{x}^{4}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4x+4y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401244} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5ced82-50c7-5506-b6d6-5d386e556ca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0adb517f-11c6-5796-89c8-e402d79f5011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401243} :answer-field/id #uuid "63dd6eb1-0216-5ca0-946f-dc8a7cfe578a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5d98cd9-310e-5d64-ad53-16fad07f2de6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161320" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}z,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-2x)}^{2}+{(-2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(-{x}^{4}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{4})\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55cd2fdb-4cae-5f0b-bea0-e845d51190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Over a Surface as a Repeated Integral Using a Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b6ce404-ebcc-51cc-943b-4e4e79eb614d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2R$ and $-2P$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401248} {:answer/id #uuid "44cf614b-483e-57c6-b48c-bdea43bdf8c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401249} {:answer/id #uuid "920e8870-c9e3-5c16-bd67-bc6987066749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $-6R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401250} {:answer/id #uuid "7051dd66-a8a1-5bd2-a47a-cbbfa2e9ad6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $-R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401251} {:answer/id #uuid "23d98ea3-b274-5a12-a721-30dfede024ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $6R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23d98ea3-b274-5a12-a721-30dfede024ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $6R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401252} :answer-field/id #uuid "d028cbae-7e56-54b5-84a6-c407d88b99d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "75438633-2fed-5dc6-8f93-bd3571d872a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161455" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $y=2x{z}^{3}$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\boxed{}\\,{z}^{3}+Q-\\boxed{}\\,x{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $j$-component.*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=2x{z}^{3},$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,z)=⟨x,\\,2x{z}^{3},z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,2{z}^{3},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,6x{z}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2{z}^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6x{z}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨2{z}^{3},\\,-1,\\,6x{z}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′}=-{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′}=⟨-2{z}^{3},\\,1,\\,-6x{z}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,R\\,⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{z}^{3},\\,1,\\,-6x{z}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2P\\,{z}^{3}+Q-6R\\,x{z}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55cd2fdb-4cae-5f0b-bea0-e845d51190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Over a Surface as a Repeated Integral Using a Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4fc5fe-cb91-578b-8dbf-4a5ffd8c3fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }-Q,\\text{ }∂Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401255} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9077fe-1593-50c2-8f89-3d6b0dcabdfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }Q,\\text{ }∂z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401256} {:answer/id #uuid "1c8485ee-e4b0-5c7e-96b8-4bdcc10e86bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }-Q,\\text{ }∂z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401257} {:answer/id #uuid "09583e22-9aba-582a-80e6-e8808ccbeebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }-Q,\\text{ }∂y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401258} {:answer/id #uuid "3581b172-6284-5dbd-9767-909757324a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }Q,\\text{ }∂y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3581b172-6284-5dbd-9767-909757324a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }Q,\\text{ }∂y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401259} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee6b27a-bcd5-52e8-9493-e839011ba707" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "417f7e3b-659c-5f7d-913c-decf384148f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161457" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{}{∂x}-\\frac{∂g}{}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55cd2fdb-4cae-5f0b-bea0-e845d51190e1" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Over a Surface as a Repeated Integral Using a Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ffab214-a531-5f16-9548-4aba64a14e09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂z$ and $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401262} {:answer/id #uuid "27be7768-bf01-5a25-8f76-a7b3aa6827e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401263} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0b36ca-8d50-5d13-b2d4-01dff2d1189e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂y$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401264} {:answer/id #uuid "8c295efa-f53d-5757-9ea8-624ce01afe74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x$ and $∂y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401265} {:answer/id #uuid "401f8a2b-7596-5052-8ec8-449369280a45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂y$ and $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c295efa-f53d-5757-9ea8-624ce01afe74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x$ and $∂y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401265} :answer-field/id #uuid "0585e533-2d5e-5fa5-b53e-85ce1c70e498" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2128236-dd4e-5cec-8d28-c07554df0f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161463" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{\\boxed{}}-Q\\frac{∂g}{\\boxed{}}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaa6b152-86d3-5f96-8a94-57f86a17a84d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Flux Over a Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aab621f-8cde-5ff7-83da-d05fc13356ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401269} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1077bb-f87e-5cca-9aef-16e4fb33aef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+e-\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401270} {:answer/id #uuid "2adea1b0-35c6-5e84-940d-c02c34a5479d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401271} {:answer/id #uuid "6170ad11-316c-53bb-b473-67e1f8f56f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401272} {:answer/id #uuid "cccdc272-4ba4-53bf-8e7e-06633eea609a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e-\\frac{13}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2adea1b0-35c6-5e84-940d-c02c34a5479d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401271} :answer-field/id #uuid "67d473e4-fb62-570e-a504-252ea46fe0d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "682d2ef7-9cff-5a52-921d-70cd49bbb080" :question/math-academy-id "q-161476" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x-y}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}{e}^{x-y}={e}^{x-y} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}{e}^{x-y}=-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩{|}_{z={e}^{x-y}} \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{-(x-y)},\\,xy{e}^{-(x-y)},\\,{e}^{x-y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{{e}^{y-x}}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{xy{e}^{y-x}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{{e}^{x-y}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-{e}^{y-x}\\cdot {e}^{x-y}-(xy{e}^{y-x})\\cdot (-{e}^{x-y})+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+xy+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-1+xy+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-y+\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}-{e}^{x-y}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-1+{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-x+{e}^{x}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{8}-1+e)-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{8}+e\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "388d2b25-435c-5296-9aa6-b2d86268ab07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<y\\le x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401276} {:answer/id #uuid "f7697d7e-3437-54cb-8f6c-2a92ca54ced0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\le y\\le x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401277} {:answer/id #uuid "715a11d5-48d4-58bf-b2c8-7bc968bb12ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x\\le y<x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401278} {:answer/id #uuid "6585c463-6545-5cb0-b223-cfd5eb13f4f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x<y<x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401279} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc09c1e-549e-5605-9dc7-40a23cf11591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<y<x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6585c463-6545-5cb0-b223-cfd5eb13f4f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x<y<x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401279} :answer-field/id #uuid "41473911-1989-5683-9728-285b3c146754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cae3814-ea99-54e9-86e7-c0bc019bdf37" :question/math-academy-id "q-161718" :question/problem "![](images/af/af1e6e7b384732a527fc77e9729c6e02aaa288d4076c030c9fae58742cb10f74.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<2,\\,}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a0b92621d83d6395926a75b5cae34f479a8b9e64dfe82711094c3724b3b3220.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/3d/3ddef68bf4b4284c1b68896920fddb8d7fb0e70eba15dee103cdbc0134a9ed20.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<2,\\,-x<y<x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f14d31-90ea-5326-97dc-45022d0835ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u+v-2w=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401283} {:answer/id #uuid "b740b568-485c-5311-a423-33ea2f3eeffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the elliptic cylinder ${u}^{2}+2{w}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401284} {:answer/id #uuid "a6795027-5482-5bff-8214-2dd2cc097fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v={u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401285} {:answer/id #uuid "5adbc1f1-362c-537c-bb50-2cdecc09b123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$ plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401286} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a89ced-7070-57be-88cf-4d4b894bd3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=2w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3a89ced-7070-57be-88cf-4d4b894bd3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=2w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401287} :answer-field/id #uuid "65676948-b2fa-585d-8619-ca89f50c6f34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8752c18-2285-5bd2-a3b2-ba4d0420bd04" :question/math-academy-id "q-161814" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+2w \\\\ v-u \\\\ uw\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+2w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v-u,\\,z(u,v,w)=uw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ w & 0 & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ w & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(u-0)+2(0-w) \\\\  & =u-2w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ u-2w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u-2w=0$ defines the plane $u=2w.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b76c85a4-c631-5c36-941d-c14613479d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401290} {:answer/id #uuid "10a3627e-14c0-553d-8005-b8e65337d0b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w=-2{v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401291} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ffd8d2-f852-57f7-9970-8454309f0281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $2u+v+w=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401292} {:answer/id #uuid "a8fe9b5d-4fbe-56d7-9627-c5c1df64a715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=-2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401293} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cbb05e-806b-56aa-8136-04b088a6dc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w={u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b76c85a4-c631-5c36-941d-c14613479d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401290} :answer-field/id #uuid "175d9200-8100-5e54-b984-b7451df1967c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc380b3f-d92e-5c45-8651-fac240787b92" :question/math-academy-id "q-161816" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{u+w} \\\\ uv+w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={e}^{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={e}^{u+w},\\,z(u,v,w)=uv+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u+w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{u+w} & 0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ v & u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}|\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}(0-u{e}^{u+w}) \\\\  & =-u{e}^{2u+w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -u{e}^{2u+w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane."}]}
